<%# Jobs index — TWO views of the same list, one page.
    The TABLE is the original and is unchanged: it is what the row-click script,
    the alignment tests and the preview-routing tests all read. The GRID is
    ADDED beside it, never a rewrite of it, and the two share the model's
    decisions (Job#preview_file / #preview_from_upload?) and the ONE favourite
    partial — a second copy of either is the thing that drifts.

    Both views are always rendered; #jobs-grid carries `hidden` in the markup,
    so the table is what shows with JS off. The CSS `[hidden]` guard
    (display: none !important) is what actually hides the inactive one — every
    .btn here sets its own display, which would otherwise beat the attribute's
    UA rule. %>
<div class="page-head">
  <div class="page-head-row page-head-row--split page-head-row--stacked">
    <h1 class="section-title"><span class="gradient-text">Jobs</span></h1>
    <%= link_to "Run Job", new_job_path, class: "btn btn-primary head-action" %>

  <%# #jobs-view-toggle-list-grid: which of the two views below is shown. Two
      plain buttons in a segmented box, not a select and not a link: the choice
      is a display mode, so it must not navigate and must not reload the page.
      They carry NO .head-action class on purpose — that class is the head's
      measured 2.75rem/7.325rem action box (see head_action tests), and these two
      are small paired controls, not head actions.

      The grid places them itself: the row is `1fr auto`, the title is pinned to
      row 1 col 1 and the blurb to row 2 col 1, so the auto-placed Run Job takes
      row 1 col 2 and this takes row 2 col 2 — directly under the Run Job button,
      at the right edge. Giving it an explicit row/column instead would put it in
      the SAME cell as Run Job, and squeezing it beside that button would mean
      widening the 1fr/auto grid the other seven heads share. %>
  <%# The wrapper closes inline on the last button's line, never as a line of its
      own: page_head_uniformity/responsive_head read the head row with a lazy
      /<div class="page-head-row[^"]*">.*?\n  <\/div>/m, so a line that begins
      with two spaces and </div> ends "the row" early and the blurb would be
      read as outside it. %>
  <div class="view-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Jobs view"><button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary view-toggle-btn" data-view-toggle="list" aria-pressed="true" title="List view — the table">List</button><button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary view-toggle-btn" data-view-toggle="grid" aria-pressed="false" title="Grid view — thumbnails">Grid</button></div>

  <p class="page-head-blurb page-head-blurb--long">Every run traces a raster into candidates, measures printability and pixel fidelity, then waits for you to pick. Nothing advances on its own — the trace stage stops at the menu, because choosing which candidate goes to final is a visual call a machine has no business making for you.</p>
  </div></div>

<%# #jobs-list-view: the table, wrapped so the toggle has one element to hide. %>
<div id="jobs-list">
<% if @jobs.any? %>
  <div class="tbl-wrap tbl-wrap--narrow">
    <table class="candidates">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="col-thumb">Preview</th>
          <th>Name</th>
          <th class="col-center">State</th>
          <th class="col-center">Passed</th>
          <th class="col-center">Runner</th>
          <th class="col-narrow" aria-label="Actions"></th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <%= render partial: "jobs/row", collection: @jobs, as: :job %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
<% else %>
  <div class="card empty-state">
    <p>No runs yet. Feed it a raster to get started.</p>
  </div>
<% end %>
</div>

<%# #jobs-grid-view: the same runs as cards -- one thumbnail, the name, the
    state and the passed count, plus the action icons. Deliberately LOW
    information: no runner column and no headers (the table is where you read
    those), so a card stays a picture you recognise rather than a paragraph.
    Hidden in the markup so a JS-less page shows the table, and so the default
    is 'list' without the script having to run. %>
<div id="jobs-grid" hidden>
<% if @jobs.any? %>
  <div class="job-grid">
    <%= render partial: "jobs/card", collection: @jobs, as: :job %>
  </div>
<% else %>
  <%# Only ever visible with the grid selected, because the list's copy of this
      card is hidden at the same moment. Two copies, one shown at a time, so the
      empty page says the same thing in either view. %>
  <div class="card empty-state">
    <p>No runs yet. Feed it a raster to get started.</p>
  </div>
<% end %>
</div>

<%# #jobs-scroll-sentinel: the infinite scroll's one piece of state. It carries
    the NEXT page to fetch, so the script holds no counter of its own -- the
    server owns the pagination and the page number travels with the markup it
    was rendered into. data-next-page is absent when the list is exhausted, and
    that absence is the ONLY stop condition: no client-side counting that can
    disagree with the controller's.

    The <a> is the no-JS fallback and stays in the DOM: a browser with no
    IntersectionObserver, or with JS off entirely, still reaches every run by
    following it. The script hides it once it takes over, so the two never
    both offer the same page. %>
<div id="jobs-scroll" class="jobs-scroll"
     <% if @has_more %>data-next-page="<%= @page + 1 %>"<% end %>>
  <div id="jobs-scroll-status" class="jobs-scroll-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <% if @has_more %>
    <a class="btn btn-secondary jobs-scroll-more" rel="next"
       href="<%= jobs_path(page: @page + 1) %>">Load more runs</a>
  <% end %>
</div>

<script>
  document.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
    if (e.target.closest('a,button,form,input,select,textarea')) return;
    var row = e.target.closest('tr.rowlink');
    if (row) window.location = row.dataset.href;
  });
</script>

<%# #jobs-infinite-scroll: appends the next page when the sentinel comes into
    view, in BOTH views at once (the table's tbody and the grid's .job-grid),
    because the head toggle swaps the wrappers and never refetches -- a run has
    to exist in whichever view you are looking at, and the two are the same
    page's data.

    It re-fetches THE SAME URL and lifts the rows and cards out of the returned
    markup by their container ids, rather than asking the server for a second,
    simpler shape. One template renders both the first page and every later
    one, so the appended markup cannot drift from the markup that was already
    there -- there is no second renderer to keep in step.

    `loading` is the in-flight latch, and it is set BEFORE the fetch and cleared
    in a finally: an IntersectionObserver fires again immediately if the
    sentinel is still on screen, and without the latch a tall page would fire
    every request at once. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    var sentinel = document.getElementById('jobs-scroll');
    if (!sentinel) return;
    // No IntersectionObserver means no automatic loading; the "Load more runs"
    // link is still there and still works, so the page degrades to paging
    // rather than to a truncated list.
    if (!window.IntersectionObserver) return;

    var status = document.getElementById('jobs-scroll-status');
    var more = sentinel.querySelector('.jobs-scroll-more');
    var loading = false;

    function say(text) { if (status) status.textContent = text || ''; }

    function appendPage(html) {
      var doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, 'text/html');
      var tbody = document.querySelector('#jobs-list tbody');
      var grid = document.querySelector('#jobs-grid .job-grid');
      var added = 0;
      if (tbody) {
        var rows = doc.querySelectorAll('#jobs-list tbody tr');
        rows.forEach(function (row) { tbody.appendChild(row); added++; });
      }
      if (grid) {
        var cards = doc.querySelectorAll('#jobs-grid .job-card');
        cards.forEach(function (card) { grid.appendChild(card); added++; });
      }
      return added;
    }

    function loadNext() {
      var next = sentinel.getAttribute('data-next-page');
      if (!next || loading) return;
      loading = true;
      say('Loading more runs…');
      var url = '/jobs?page=' + encodeURIComponent(next);
      fetch(url, { headers: { 'Accept': 'text/html' }, credentials: 'same-origin' })
        .then(function (res) {
          if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
          return res.text();
        })
        .then(function (html) {
          appendPage(html);
          say('');
          // The NEXT page number comes from the response's own sentinel, so the
          // server stays the only thing that decides when the list ends.
          var parsed = new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, 'text/html');
          var nextSentinel = parsed.getElementById('jobs-scroll');
          var following = nextSentinel && nextSentinel.getAttribute('data-next-page');
          if (following) {
            sentinel.setAttribute('data-next-page', following);
            if (more) more.setAttribute('href', '/jobs?page=' + encodeURIComponent(following));
          } else {
            sentinel.removeAttribute('data-next-page');
            if (more) more.remove();
            say('That is every run.');
          }
          if (window.csRewireDynamic) window.csRewireDynamic();
        })
        .catch(function () {
          say('Could not load more runs — use “Load more runs” to retry.');
        })
        .finally(function () { loading = false; });
    }

    if (!window.__csJobsScroll) {
      window.__csJobsScroll = true;
      // The observer is created ONCE and delegated from document, so it
      // survives Turbo's body swap: a per-visit observer would stack, and a
      // stale one would hold a sentinel that is no longer in the document.
      window.__csJobsScrollObserver =
        new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
          entries.forEach(function (entry) {
            if (entry.isIntersecting) loadNext();
          });
        }, { rootMargin: '600px 0px' });
      document.addEventListener('turbo:load', function () {
        var s = document.getElementById('jobs-scroll');
        if (s && s.hasAttribute('data-next-page')) window.__csJobsScrollObserver.observe(s);
      });
    }
    // The fallback link is hidden once the observer owns the sentinel; both
    // offering the same page is the duplication, not the safety net.
    if (more && sentinel.hasAttribute('data-next-page')) {
      more.hidden = true;
      window.__csJobsScrollObserver.observe(sentinel);
    }
  })();
</script>

<%# #jobs-view-toggle-script: the toggle's whole implementation.
    - The class on <body> is the mode, so CSS can key off it; the `hidden`
      attribute on the two wrappers is what actually swaps them.
    - localStorage keeps the choice across visits. It is read with a guard and
      written with a guard: a private-mode browser throws on both, and the page
      must still work (it just forgets).
    - The click listener is bound ONCE and DELEGATED from document, so it
      survives Turbo Drive's body swap (this script re-runs on every visit, and
      a per-visit binding would stack listeners). apply() re-runs on turbo:load
      because the swapped-in body starts in the markup's default state.
    - Default is 'list': nothing here runs and the table is already showing. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    var KEY = 'chopshop.jobsView';

    function readView() {
      try { return localStorage.getItem(KEY) === 'grid' ? 'grid' : 'list'; }
      catch (e) { return 'list'; }
    }

    function apply(view) {
      var grid = view === 'grid';
      var list = document.getElementById('jobs-list');
      var cards = document.getElementById('jobs-grid');
      if (list) list.hidden = grid;
      if (cards) cards.hidden = !grid;
      document.body.classList.toggle('jobs-view-grid', grid);
      document.querySelectorAll('[data-view-toggle]').forEach(function (btn) {
        var on = (btn.dataset.viewToggle === 'grid') === grid;
        btn.classList.toggle('is-active', on);
        btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', on ? 'true' : 'false');
      });
    }

    function writeView(view) {
      try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, view); } catch (e) { /* forget it, show it */ }
      apply(view);
    }

    if (!window.__csJobsView) {
      window.__csJobsView = true;
      document.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
        var btn = e.target.closest('[data-view-toggle]');
        if (!btn) return;
        e.preventDefault();
        writeView(btn.dataset.viewToggle === 'grid' ? 'grid' : 'list');
      });
      document.addEventListener('turbo:load', function () { apply(readView()); });
    }

    apply(readView());
  })();
</script>
